# XCU date
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tests run in the POSIX locale and with a TZ the test chooses. The
# time-setting operand (XSI) is not exercised: it changes the system clock.

assertion  XCU.date.001
anchor     utilities/date.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/date.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       With no operand, in the POSIX locale, date writes the same text
           as date with the format operand "+%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y".
test       t/001-default-format.sh

assertion  XCU.date.002
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       The output for a +format operand is the formatted text followed by
           one newline: a bare "+" gives only a newline, ordinary text is
           copied, %n gives a newline, %t a tab and %% a percent sign, and a
           newline is still added after a format that ends in %n.
test       t/002-format-newline.sh

assertion  XCU.date.003
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       Within one invocation, the compound conversions agree with their
           definitions in the POSIX locale: %D with %m/%d/%y, %T with
           %H:%M:%S, %R with %H:%M, %F with %Y-%m-%d, %h with %b, %C%y with %Y,
           %r with %I:%M:%S %p, %x with %m/%d/%y, %X with %H:%M:%S and %c with
           %a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y.
test       t/003-compound-conversions.sh

assertion  XCU.date.004
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       In the POSIX locale %a, %A, %b, %B and %p give the English names
           and AM or PM, the numeric conversions stay in their ranges (%H,
           %I, %M, %S, %d, %m, %j, %u, %w, %U, %W, %V), %e is %d with a
           leading zero replaced by a space, and the weekday name, %w, %u, %H,
           %I and %p agree with each other.
test       t/004-names-and-ranges.sh

assertion  XCU.date.005
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       With -u the time is shown as UTC whatever TZ holds: with TZ set to
           an offset zone, "date -u +%H%M" equals the same conversion run with
           TZ=UTC0, and %Z is UTC or GMT.
test       t/005-utc-option.sh

assertion  XCU.date.006
anchor     utilities/date.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       Without -u the zone in TZ is used: with TZ=XYZ5, %Z is XYZ, %z is
           -0500 and the hour is five hours behind the hour shown for UTC.
test       t/006-tz-honoured.sh

assertion  XCU.date.007
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/date.html#EXIT STATUS
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       A time-setting operand whose month is 13 cannot be applied: date
           exits with a status above zero.
test       t/007-bad-set-operand.sh

assertion  XCU.date.008
anchor     utilities/date.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/date.html#STDERR
class      required
says       A successful "date +%Y" exits with status zero, writes a four digit
           year to standard output and nothing to standard error.
test       t/008-status-and-stderr.sh

assertion  XCU.date.009
anchor     utilities/date.html#OPERANDS
class      untestable
when       XSI
says       The mmddhhmm[[cc]yy] operand sets the system date and time, with
           two-digit years 69 to 99 meaning 1969 to 1999 and 00 to 68 meaning
           2000 to 2068, the current year when the year is omitted.
reason     Setting the system clock changes the state of the machine under
           test and needs privilege; the effect cannot be undone exactly.
